### Page 6 of 14

```text
PUDC-23-106

October 2, 2023
2) The application reflects sound planning and urban design principles with respect to existing
and potential future development in the vicinity, including consideration of current and future
streets, open spaces, blocks and lots, and public utilities or facilities. (UDC 2.1.2.d.2)

The proposed application combines properties to create one larger lot. The surrounding area is
already developed as residential.  There are existing facilities.

The application complies with this review criterion.

3) The division of land meets the purposes, intent, and all applicable standards in Article 4. (UDC
2.1.2.d.3)

Community Facilities and Fees (4.2):
The proposed subdivision is a replat eliminating interior lots lines and there is not an
associated zone change.  This development is exempt from Community Facility Fees. (UDC
4.2.5.c.6 (a) and(c).

Transportation Network (4.3):
The plat should dedicate 10’ of right-of-way along Ridge Road as it is classified as a Minor
Arterial and requires a 100’ right-of-way and is currently an 80’ right-of-way.  This plat is
occurring within an area of the community which has largely been previously developed.  An
exception for block sizes must be granted.  The existing block size perimeter is about 3,500’
and the maximum block size perimeter is 2,200’.  Most of the block is already built out with
existing residential and any proposed roads would likely not meet City requirements and
would require separate exception to code.

Open Space (4.4):
Open space is required at 750sqft per unit or 8% of the gross development area, whichever
is less. However, due to the nature of this application, plat-level open space is not required.
The open space requirement will be met during the site plan process.

Required Improvements (4.5):
The streets adjacent to the plat to the east (Ridge Road) and south (Jackson Street) are in
need of improvement of curb, gutter and sidewalks. Utilities are existing.  These
improvements will be required during the site plan process.

Public Safety Fees (4.6):
Public Safety Fees will be due with building permits of new construction on the site.

The application complies with this review criterion, with a condition and an exception.

4) Any phasing proposed in the application is clearly indicated and demonstrates a logical and
well-planned development pattern. This may include consideration of amenities and public
facilities of the subdivision within each phase compared to the anticipated timing or the
likelihood of future phases being constructed as planned. (UDC 2.1.2.d.4)

No phasing is proposed with this project.

Review criterion not applicable.
```

### Page 7 of 14

```text
PUDC-23-106

October 2, 2023
5) The impacts identified in specific studies or technical reports that were prepared or requested
to be prepared in association with the application, and whether the findings and
recommendations of these reports, or any modifications or conditions on approval of the
application, can mitigate these impacts. (UDC 2.1.2.d.5)

Drainage:
A drainage analysis was submitted and reviewed. There were comments regarding the need for
a final drainage plan for the site which will need to address mitigation requirements.  City Staff
and the applicant anticipate addressing the specific issues with a site plan and engineering plan
review submittal.

Traffic:
A traffic study was submitted and reviewed. There were no concerns with traffic at the plat level
for the project as presented.  A couple of traffic concerns may be addressed with the site plan.

The application complies with this review criterion.

6) Lots proposed for development shall be buildable lots according to the existing or proposed
zoning district standards, result in design and development patterns that meet the Intent and
Applicability statements of the existing or proposed zoning district, and not aggravate
nonconforming setbacks or currently adopted building and fire code access and separation
requirements. (UDC 2.1.2.d.6)

The proposed plat proposes one lot. The lot is buildable in the proposed zone district. The
existing structures meet setbacks and do not aggravate existing adopted building and fire access
and separation requirements.

The application complies with this review criterion.

7) All parcels not proposed as buildable lots and which are not dedicated to and accepted by the
City shall have a clearly defined entity responsible for ongoing maintenance.  (UDC 2.1.2.d.7)

The plat proposes one lot for a school. The Plat does not propose any lots subject to this
criterion.

Review criterion not applicable.

8) The application will not create any undue influence on existing or potential development for
adjacent property considering the existing zoning and Comprehensive Plan policies for that
property.  (UDC 2.1.2.d.8)

The plat is adjacent to streets on the south and east. Surrounding properties are largely already
developed with residential.

The application complies with this review criterion.

9) The application is not harmful to the public health, safety, and welfare. (UDC 2.1.2.d.9)

City staff is satisfied that this proposed plat would not adversely affect the public health, safety,
or general welfare.
